          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-201",
              "name": "CVE-2026-92708: devalue 5.9.2 process memory disclosure via typed-array serialization (transitive via astro)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-92708: devalue 5.9.2 process memory disclosure via typed-array serialization (transitive via astro)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The dependency tree pinned by `bun.lock` contains **devalue 5.9.2**, which is affected by **CVE-2026-92708** (published advisory CVSS 7.5 HIGH): `stringify`/`uneval` serialize a typed array by emitting its entire backing `ArrayBuffer`, so serializing a Node `Buffer` (backing store is a process-wide shared pool) discloses up to 64 KB of unrelated process memory into the serialized output. Fixed in **5.9.3**. devalue is a transitive dependency pulled in by `astro@7.3.2` (`bun.lock:612`, dependency `\"devalue\": \"^5.8.1\"`; pinned at `bun.lock:778`). In this repository the exposure is minimal: the site is a fully prerendered static Astro build with no SSR server, and no project- or CMS-controlled data reaches the vulnerable typed-array serialization path."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The dependency tree pinned by `bun.lock` contains **devalue 5.9.2**, which is affected by **CVE-2026-92708** (published advisory CVSS 7.5 HIGH): `stringify`/`uneval` serialize a typed array by emitting its entire backing `ArrayBuffer`, so serializing a Node `Buffer` (backing store is a process-wide shared pool) discloses up to 64 KB of unrelated process memory into the serialized output. Fixed in **5.9.3**. devalue is a transitive dependency pulled in by `astro@7.3.2` (`bun.lock:612`, dependency `\"devalue\": \"^5.8.1\"`; pinned at `bun.lock:778`). In this repository the exposure is minimal: the site is a fully prerendered static Astro build with no SSR server, and no project- or CMS-controlled data reaches the vulnerable typed-array serialization path.\n\nBuild-process memory disclosure: if a Node Buffer or typed array were passed through devalue serialization during `astro build`, up to 64 KB of build-process memory (potentially including build env such as `CONTENT_API_KEY`/`GHOST_URL` set in the Docker build stage) would be emitted into published static output. In this codebase no Buffer/typed-array data is serialized and no long-running multi-request SSR process exists, so no disclosure was demonstrated; the pinned version nonetheless keeps the vulnerable code in the build toolchain.\n\nRefresh the lockfile so the transitive resolution moves devalue to the fixed release: run `bun update devalue` (astro's range `^5.8.1` accepts 5.9.3, so no manifest change is required). If a future astro release re-pins or the resolution stays stale, add a root-level `overrides` entry pinning `devalue` to `^5.9.3` in package.json. Also apply the sibling fixes bundled in devalue 5.9.3 (six GHSA-only advisories covering uneval expansion, ArrayBuffer-reviver validation, unhandled stringifyAsync rejection, `__proto__` key coercion, and sparse-array amplification — not separately filed because they have no CVE IDs). Re-run `bun run build` and confirm the lockfile records `devalue@5.9.3`.",
                "markdown": "The dependency tree pinned by `bun.lock` contains **devalue 5.9.2**, which is affected by **CVE-2026-92708** (published advisory CVSS 7.5 HIGH): `stringify`/`uneval` serialize a typed array by emitting its entire backing `ArrayBuffer`, so serializing a Node `Buffer` (backing store is a process-wide shared pool) discloses up to 64 KB of unrelated process memory into the serialized output. Fixed in **5.9.3**. devalue is a transitive dependency pulled in by `astro@7.3.2` (`bun.lock:612`, dependency `\"devalue\": \"^5.8.1\"`; pinned at `bun.lock:778`). In this repository the exposure is minimal: the site is a fully prerendered static Astro build with no SSR server, and no project- or CMS-controlled data reaches the vulnerable typed-array serialization path.\n\nBuild-process memory disclosure: if a Node Buffer or typed array were passed through devalue serialization during `astro build`, up to 64 KB of build-process memory (potentially including build env such as `CONTENT_API_KEY`/`GHOST_URL` set in the Docker build stage) would be emitted into published static output. In this codebase no Buffer/typed-array data is serialized and no long-running multi-request SSR process exists, so no disclosure was demonstrated; the pinned version nonetheless keeps the vulnerable code in the build toolchain.\n\nRefresh the lockfile so the transitive resolution moves devalue to the fixed release: run `bun update devalue` (astro's range `^5.8.1` accepts 5.9.3, so no manifest change is required). If a future astro release re-pins or the resolution stays stale, add a root-level `overrides` entry pinning `devalue` to `^5.9.3` in package.json. Also apply the sibling fixes bundled in devalue 5.9.3 (six GHSA-only advisories covering uneval expansion, ArrayBuffer-reviver validation, unhandled stringifyAsync rejection, `__proto__` key coercion, and sparse-array amplification — not separately filed because they have no CVE IDs). Re-run `bun run build` and confirm the lockfile records `devalue@5.9.3`."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "1.9",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-201",
                  "CVE-2026-92708",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/201.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-524",
              "name": "CVE-2026-93748: http-cache-semantics 4.2.0 cross-user cache disclosure via max-stale (transitive via astro)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-93748: http-cache-semantics 4.2.0 cross-user cache disclosure via max-stale (transitive via astro)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `bun.lock` tree pins **http-cache-semantics 4.2.0**, affected by **CVE-2026-93748** (published advisory CVSS 7.5 HIGH): when processing client `max-stale` directives the library fails to properly validate security-zeroed cache entries, letting unauthenticated attackers retrieve cached responses belonging to other users (e.g. another user's `Set-Cookie` credentials) from a shared cache. The advisory covers all versions through 4.2.0 and no fixed release is listed. The package is transitive via `astro@7.3.2` (`bun.lock:612`, `\"http-cache-semantics\": \"^4.2.0\"`; pin at `bun.lock:900`). This deployment has no shared cache, no per-user server responses, and never loads the sole consumer module, so the published rating does not carry over to this codebase."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `bun.lock` tree pins **http-cache-semantics 4.2.0**, affected by **CVE-2026-93748** (published advisory CVSS 7.5 HIGH): when processing client `max-stale` directives the library fails to properly validate security-zeroed cache entries, letting unauthenticated attackers retrieve cached responses belonging to other users (e.g. another user's `Set-Cookie` credentials) from a shared cache. The advisory covers all versions through 4.2.0 and no fixed release is listed. The package is transitive via `astro@7.3.2` (`bun.lock:612`, `\"http-cache-semantics\": \"^4.2.0\"`; pin at `bun.lock:900`). This deployment has no shared cache, no per-user server responses, and never loads the sole consumer module, so the published rating does not carry over to this codebase.\n\nNo impact demonstrated in this codebase. The vulnerable behavior would allow cross-user disclosure of cached HTTP responses (including session cookies) from a shared cache; here the only consumer is astro's build-time remote-image caching, the project uses no remote images, and the site serves identical static files to every visitor, so there are no per-user cached responses and no attacker-reachable cache query path. Risk is latent: a future feature that enables remote images or server-side/shared caching would pull this code into an active path while the version remains unfixed upstream.\n\nNo fixed release exists upstream at scan time. Track kornelski/http-cache-semantics issue #56 and bump via `bun update http-cache-semantics` (or a root package.json `overrides` entry pinning the first fixed version) once a release lands; astro's range `^4.2.0` will accept it. Also upgrade `astro` when a release ships with the fixed dependency. As defense in depth, avoid enabling astro remote-image fetching or any server-side/shared response caching for this site without first confirming the dependency has been fixed; if remote images are ever introduced, verify no cache is shared across users.",
                "markdown": "The `bun.lock` tree pins **http-cache-semantics 4.2.0**, affected by **CVE-2026-93748** (published advisory CVSS 7.5 HIGH): when processing client `max-stale` directives the library fails to properly validate security-zeroed cache entries, letting unauthenticated attackers retrieve cached responses belonging to other users (e.g. another user's `Set-Cookie` credentials) from a shared cache. The advisory covers all versions through 4.2.0 and no fixed release is listed. The package is transitive via `astro@7.3.2` (`bun.lock:612`, `\"http-cache-semantics\": \"^4.2.0\"`; pin at `bun.lock:900`). This deployment has no shared cache, no per-user server responses, and never loads the sole consumer module, so the published rating does not carry over to this codebase.\n\nNo impact demonstrated in this codebase. The vulnerable behavior would allow cross-user disclosure of cached HTTP responses (including session cookies) from a shared cache; here the only consumer is astro's build-time remote-image caching, the project uses no remote images, and the site serves identical static files to every visitor, so there are no per-user cached responses and no attacker-reachable cache query path. Risk is latent: a future feature that enables remote images or server-side/shared caching would pull this code into an active path while the version remains unfixed upstream.\n\nNo fixed release exists upstream at scan time. Track kornelski/http-cache-semantics issue #56 and bump via `bun update http-cache-semantics` (or a root package.json `overrides` entry pinning the first fixed version) once a release lands; astro's range `^4.2.0` will accept it. Also upgrade `astro` when a release ships with the fixed dependency. As defense in depth, avoid enabling astro remote-image fetching or any server-side/shared response caching for this site without first confirming the dependency has been fixed; if remote images are ever introduced, verify no cache is shared across users."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-524",
                  "CVE-2026-93748",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/524.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-407",
              "name": "CVE-2026-104844: postcss-selector-parser 6.0.10 quadratic-time DoS via crafted CSS selectors (transitive via @tailwindcss/typography)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-104844: postcss-selector-parser 6.0.10 quadratic-time DoS via crafted CSS selectors (transitive via @tailwindcss/typography)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `bun.lock` tree pins **postcss-selector-parser 6.0.10**, affected by **CVE-2026-104844** (published advisory CVSS 5.9 MEDIUM): `splitWord()` can receive a flat selector as one word token carrying many class/ID indexes, and repeated deduplication and membership scans make parsing quadratic in the number of indexes, allowing a crafted selector to cause prolonged CPU consumption (denial of service). Versions before 7.1.6 are affected; the fix is 7.1.6. The package is a transitive, exactly-pinned dependency of **@tailwindcss/typography 0.5.20** (`bun.lock:422`, `\"postcss-selector-parser\": \"6.0.10\"`), which is a direct dependency of this project (`package.json:20`) and active via `@plugin \"@tailwindcss/typography\"` in `src/styles/globals.css:3`. The vulnerable parser runs on every build, but its input is entirely developer-authored CSS — no attacker-controlled data reaches it in this codebase."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `bun.lock` tree pins **postcss-selector-parser 6.0.10**, affected by **CVE-2026-104844** (published advisory CVSS 5.9 MEDIUM): `splitWord()` can receive a flat selector as one word token carrying many class/ID indexes, and repeated deduplication and membership scans make parsing quadratic in the number of indexes, allowing a crafted selector to cause prolonged CPU consumption (denial of service). Versions before 7.1.6 are affected; the fix is 7.1.6. The package is a transitive, exactly-pinned dependency of **@tailwindcss/typography 0.5.20** (`bun.lock:422`, `\"postcss-selector-parser\": \"6.0.10\"`), which is a direct dependency of this project (`package.json:20`) and active via `@plugin \"@tailwindcss/typography\"` in `src/styles/globals.css:3`. The vulnerable parser runs on every build, but its input is entirely developer-authored CSS — no attacker-controlled data reaches it in this codebase.\n\nBuild-time denial of service on the operator's machine or CI: a single crafted CSS selector (a class or ID list with quadratic index growth) committed to a scanned source file could block `astro build` for an extended period. No confidentiality or integrity impact, and no impact on the deployed static site or its visitors — the parser never runs at request time. Reaching the input requires the ability to write to the repository (or otherwise control files Tailwind scans), which already implies the ability to break or alter the build.\n\nOverride the transitive dependency to the fixed major and verify the build still passes: add a root package.json `overrides` entry pinning `postcss-selector-parser` to `^7.1.6`, run `bun install`, then `bun run build` and visually check the blog typography (the 7.x release is a major bump — selector AST API changes could affect the plugin, so a regression check of `.prose` styling is required). Alternatively, wait for or vendor a `@tailwindcss/typography` release that updates its pinned `postcss-selector-parser` dependency (currently the exact pin `6.0.10` at bun.lock:422 prevents any non-overridden upgrade). The plugin is genuinely used (src/styles/globals.css:3), so removal is not a drop-in option.",
                "markdown": "The `bun.lock` tree pins **postcss-selector-parser 6.0.10**, affected by **CVE-2026-104844** (published advisory CVSS 5.9 MEDIUM): `splitWord()` can receive a flat selector as one word token carrying many class/ID indexes, and repeated deduplication and membership scans make parsing quadratic in the number of indexes, allowing a crafted selector to cause prolonged CPU consumption (denial of service). Versions before 7.1.6 are affected; the fix is 7.1.6. The package is a transitive, exactly-pinned dependency of **@tailwindcss/typography 0.5.20** (`bun.lock:422`, `\"postcss-selector-parser\": \"6.0.10\"`), which is a direct dependency of this project (`package.json:20`) and active via `@plugin \"@tailwindcss/typography\"` in `src/styles/globals.css:3`. The vulnerable parser runs on every build, but its input is entirely developer-authored CSS — no attacker-controlled data reaches it in this codebase.\n\nBuild-time denial of service on the operator's machine or CI: a single crafted CSS selector (a class or ID list with quadratic index growth) committed to a scanned source file could block `astro build` for an extended period. No confidentiality or integrity impact, and no impact on the deployed static site or its visitors — the parser never runs at request time. Reaching the input requires the ability to write to the repository (or otherwise control files Tailwind scans), which already implies the ability to break or alter the build.\n\nOverride the transitive dependency to the fixed major and verify the build still passes: add a root package.json `overrides` entry pinning `postcss-selector-parser` to `^7.1.6`, run `bun install`, then `bun run build` and visually check the blog typography (the 7.x release is a major bump — selector AST API changes could affect the plugin, so a regression check of `.prose` styling is required). Alternatively, wait for or vendor a `@tailwindcss/typography` release that updates its pinned `postcss-selector-parser` dependency (currently the exact pin `6.0.10` at bun.lock:422 prevents any non-overridden upgrade). The plugin is genuinely used (src/styles/globals.css:3), so removal is not a drop-in option."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "1.9",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-407",
                  "CVE-2026-104844",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/407.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-400",
              "name": "CVE-2026-93749: source-map-js 1.2.1 event-loop DoS via malformed indexed source maps (transitive build dependency)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-93749: source-map-js 1.2.1 event-loop DoS via malformed indexed source maps (transitive build dependency)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `bun.lock` tree pins **source-map-js 1.2.1**, affected by **CVE-2026-93749** (published advisory CVSS 7.5 HIGH): the library fails to validate the per-section offset line value in indexed source maps, so an attacker-supplied map with extremely large offset values causes synchronous event-loop blocking (denial of service). Versions through 1.2.1 are affected; the fix is 1.2.2. The package is a transitive build-tool dependency reached through several roots — primarily `astro@7.3.2 > vite@8.3.0 > postcss@8.5.28 > source-map-js@1.2.1` (`bun.lock:612`, `bun.lock:1238`, `bun.lock:1070`, pin at `bun.lock:1146`), and also via `tailwindcss`/`@tailwindcss/node` (bun.lock:424), `css-tree` (bun.lock:678), `magicast` (bun.lock:980), and `@vue/compiler-sfc`/`@vue/compiler-core`. This project emits no source maps and no attacker-controlled map can enter the build pipeline, so the vulnerable consumer has no reachable untrusted input here."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `bun.lock` tree pins **source-map-js 1.2.1**, affected by **CVE-2026-93749** (published advisory CVSS 7.5 HIGH): the library fails to validate the per-section offset line value in indexed source maps, so an attacker-supplied map with extremely large offset values causes synchronous event-loop blocking (denial of service). Versions through 1.2.1 are affected; the fix is 1.2.2. The package is a transitive build-tool dependency reached through several roots — primarily `astro@7.3.2 > vite@8.3.0 > postcss@8.5.28 > source-map-js@1.2.1` (`bun.lock:612`, `bun.lock:1238`, `bun.lock:1070`, pin at `bun.lock:1146`), and also via `tailwindcss`/`@tailwindcss/node` (bun.lock:424), `css-tree` (bun.lock:678), `magicast` (bun.lock:980), and `@vue/compiler-sfc`/`@vue/compiler-core`. This project emits no source maps and no attacker-controlled map can enter the build pipeline, so the vulnerable consumer has no reachable untrusted input here.\n\nBuild-time denial of service on the operator's machine or CI if a malicious indexed source map were introduced into the toolchain input (e.g. committed to the repository): a `.map` file with an extreme per-section offset line value could block the build's event loop for an extended period. No confidentiality or integrity impact, and no effect on the deployed static site or its visitors — source-map-js never runs at request time, and no `.map` files are even shipped (verified: zero `*.map` files under `dist/`). Supplying the map requires write access to the repository, a trusted position.\n\nRefresh the lockfile so the transitive resolution moves to the fixed patch: run `bun update source-map-js` — every parent range accepts 1.2.2 (`postcss` and `css-tree` require `^1.2.1`), so no manifest change is needed. Verify afterward that `bun.lock` records `source-map-js@1.2.2` and that `bun run build` still succeeds. As defense in depth, never enable `build.sourcemap` in astro.config.mjs for the production deployment, and treat any third-party `.map` file added to the repository as untrusted input.",
                "markdown": "The `bun.lock` tree pins **source-map-js 1.2.1**, affected by **CVE-2026-93749** (published advisory CVSS 7.5 HIGH): the library fails to validate the per-section offset line value in indexed source maps, so an attacker-supplied map with extremely large offset values causes synchronous event-loop blocking (denial of service). Versions through 1.2.1 are affected; the fix is 1.2.2. The package is a transitive build-tool dependency reached through several roots — primarily `astro@7.3.2 > vite@8.3.0 > postcss@8.5.28 > source-map-js@1.2.1` (`bun.lock:612`, `bun.lock:1238`, `bun.lock:1070`, pin at `bun.lock:1146`), and also via `tailwindcss`/`@tailwindcss/node` (bun.lock:424), `css-tree` (bun.lock:678), `magicast` (bun.lock:980), and `@vue/compiler-sfc`/`@vue/compiler-core`. This project emits no source maps and no attacker-controlled map can enter the build pipeline, so the vulnerable consumer has no reachable untrusted input here.\n\nBuild-time denial of service on the operator's machine or CI if a malicious indexed source map were introduced into the toolchain input (e.g. committed to the repository): a `.map` file with an extreme per-section offset line value could block the build's event loop for an extended period. No confidentiality or integrity impact, and no effect on the deployed static site or its visitors — source-map-js never runs at request time, and no `.map` files are even shipped (verified: zero `*.map` files under `dist/`). Supplying the map requires write access to the repository, a trusted position.\n\nRefresh the lockfile so the transitive resolution moves to the fixed patch: run `bun update source-map-js` — every parent range accepts 1.2.2 (`postcss` and `css-tree` require `^1.2.1`), so no manifest change is needed. Verify afterward that `bun.lock` records `source-map-js@1.2.2` and that `bun run build` still succeeds. As defense in depth, never enable `build.sourcemap` in astro.config.mjs for the production deployment, and treat any third-party `.map` file added to the repository as untrusted input."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "1.9",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-400",
                  "CVE-2026-93749",
                  "stride:D"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html"
            },
            {
