                "contextual_cvss_reasoning": "The published 7.5 (AV:N/PR:N/C:H) rates devalue inside a long-running internet-facing SSR framework where a public page returns a Buffer. This codebase proves that position does not exist: the entry point is the lockfile pin (bun.lock:778) consumed by the operator-invoked static build (`bun run build` in the Dockerfile build stage), astro's devalue call sites are limited to content collections/actions/sessions (node_modules/astro/dist/content/*, actions/runtime/*, core/session/runtime.js) — none activated here (no src/content/, static output) — and the only island props are literal `{}` (dist/index.html), so no Buffer/typed-array ever reaches `devalue.stringify`. A network attacker has no request path into this code at all; the residual trigger would require a developer (or someone who can already commit code) to pass a Buffer through island/content props, at which point up to 64 KB of build-process memory could land in published HTML — hence confidentiality limited to L, attack vector local, privileges required high, and integrity/availability none."
