              "cwe": "CWE-400",
              "impact": "Build-time denial of service on the operator's machine or CI if a malicious indexed source map were introduced into the toolchain input (e.g. committed to the repository): a `.map` file with an extreme per-section offset line value could block the build's event loop for an extended period. No confidentiality or integrity impact, and no effect on the deployed static site or its visitors — source-map-js never runs at request time, and no `.map` files are even shipped (verified: zero `*.map` files under `dist/`). Supplying the map requires write access to the repository, a trusted position.",
              "technical_analysis": "**Version verification.** `bun.lock:1146` pins `source-map-js@1.2.1`; the advisory covers through 1.2.1 with fix 1.2.2, so the pin is affected. Trivy (DB refreshed 2026-10-14) matched it from `bun.lock`.\n\n**Source-to-sink trace (this codebase).**\n- Entry points (lockfile → usage): `astro: ^7.3.2` (package.json:26) at bun.lock:612 pulls `vite ^8.0.13` (bun.lock:1238) → `postcss ^8.5.28` (bun.lock:1070) → `source-map-js ^1.2.1`; `@tailwindcss/vite` (package.json:21, bun.lock:424) → `tailwindcss` → `@tailwindcss/node` → `source-map-js ^1.2.1`; and the same package via `css-tree@3.2.1` (bun.lock:678), `magicast@0.5.5` (bun.lock:980), and `@vue/compiler-sfc`/`@vue/compiler-core` (vue 3.5.42 chain).\n- Sink: the advisory's vulnerable consumer is the indexed-source-map section walker in `lib/source-node.js` (per-section offset line handling, commit cf76580). It runs whenever a tool parses an *indexed* `.map` file.\n- Reachability in this project: none demonstrated. `astro.config.mjs` sets no `build.sourcemap`; the previously built `dist/` contains zero `*.map` files, proving the pipeline neither emits nor consumes source maps during `astro build`. The CSS pipeline (Tailwind v4 via @tailwindcss/vite) runs without sourcemap options; input CSS is developer-authored (src/styles/globals.css and scanned components). Ghost CMS content (src/lib/ghost.ts:14) is rendered into HTML via set:html and can never become a source map. The deployed site is static nginx-served files — no request-time use of this package.\n\n**Counterevidence pass.** Confirmed no source maps exist anywhere in the build output, no sourcemap option is set anywhere in astro.config.mjs or the CSS pipeline, and the only `.map`-bearing inputs an attacker could try to introduce (a committed malicious `.map`, or a `sourceMappingURL` comment in a CSS/JS source) require repository write access — a trusted developer position already able to break the build. Nothing in the runtime deployment touches this package.\n\n**Environment caveat.** Web search unavailable; CVE identity, affected range, fixed version, and CVSS taken from the Trivy DB refreshed on scan day plus its embedded references (Red Hat CVE page, upstream PR #79 / commit cf76580, VulnCheck advisory).",
              "remediation_steps": "Refresh the lockfile so the transitive resolution moves to the fixed patch: run `bun update source-map-js` — every parent range accepts 1.2.2 (`postcss` and `css-tree` require `^1.2.1`), so no manifest change is needed. Verify afterward that `bun.lock` records `source-map-js@1.2.2` and that `bun run build` still succeeds. As defense in depth, never enable `build.sourcemap` in astro.config.mjs for the production deployment, and treat any third-party `.map` file added to the repository as untrusted input.",
              "dependency_metadata": {
                "package_name": "source-map-js",
                "installed_version": "1.2.1",
                "advisory_cvss": 7.5,
                "package_ecosystem": "npm",
                "manifest_path": "bun.lock",
                "fixed_version": "1.2.2",
                "introduced_by": "astro@7.3.2",
                "dependency_path": "astro@7.3.2 > vite@8.3.0 > postcss@8.5.28 > source-map-js@1.2.1",
                "reachability": "imported",
                "reachability_evidence": "source-map-js is not imported by application code; it enters through multiple build-tool chains of direct dependencies: `astro@7.3.2 > vite@8.3.0 > postcss@8.5.28 > source-map-js@1.2.1` (package.json:26; bun.lock:612, 1238, 1070, 1146), `@tailwindcss/vite@4.3.3 > tailwindcss@4.3.3 > @tailwindcss/node@4.3.3 > source-map-js` (package.json:21; bun.lock:424), plus `css-tree@3.2.1` (bun.lock:678), `magicast@0.5.5` (bun.lock:980), and `@vue/compiler-sfc`/`@vue/compiler-core` (vue 3.5.42 chain). Usage of the affected indexed-map parsing with untrusted input was not confirmed anywhere: astro.config.mjs sets no `build.sourcemap` and the built output contains zero `*.map` files (verified with `find dist -name '*.map'`), so the build neither emits nor consumes source maps; Tailwind v4's CSS pipeline likewise runs without sourcemaps, and its file scan (developer-authored sources only, .gitignore respected — dist/ ignored at .gitignore:2) can never contain Ghost CMS-derived content. No HTTP endpoint or request-time code path uses this package — the deployment is static files behind nginx.",
                "contextual_cvss_breakdown": {
