                "contextual_cvss_reasoning": "The published 7.5 (AV:N/PR:N/A:H) assumes an attacker can make a network-reachable service parse a malicious indexed source map. In this codebase the package exists only inside the operator's build toolchain: entry points are the direct dependency pins `astro: ^7.3.2` (package.json:26) via `astro@7.3.2 > vite@8.3.0 > postcss@8.5.28 > source-map-js@1.2.1` (bun.lock:612, 1238, 1070, 1146) and `@tailwindcss/vite`/`tailwindcss` via `@tailwindcss/node` (bun.lock:424), with additional chains through css-tree, magicast, and @vue/compiler-sfc/-core. The project emits no source maps at all (no `build.sourcemap` in astro.config.mjs and zero `*.map` files in the built dist/), so the vulnerable indexed-map consumer is never fed a map in the first place — and no attacker-controlled source map can enter the pipeline: everything the toolchain could parse is self-generated or repo-committed, and Ghost CMS content never becomes a source map. A crafted map would require commit access (a trusted developer position), so attack vector is local, privileges required high, and the demonstrated impact is build-time CPU denial only (A:L)."
